Chapter: GopRtc

 The CMOS real-time clock, read correctly.

 The MC146818 updates its time registers once a second, and for the
 window in which it does so it raises Update-In-Progress (Status A,
 bit 7) and the time registers are UNDEFINED -- the spec says so, and
 real silicon duly returns junk. A reader that polls the seconds
 without checking UIP therefore sees the value change many times a
 second, and any code counting those changes as elapsed time runs
 wild: the first-boot ceremony's keyboard gate counted thirty
 "seconds" in microseconds on a real board, handed the USB controller
 back before the countdown could be seen, and in doing so switched off
 the very keyboard pump whose silence it was measuring.

 It survived two emulators to get there. Neither QEMU nor codex-vm
 modelled the update cycle: both answered every read with a clean
 value and swore no update was ever in progress. codex-vm does now.

 So this reader does three things, and a caller that skips any of
 them is not reading a clock, it is reading noise:

   1. Wait for UIP to clear, fuel-bounded, before touching a time
      register at all.
   2. Read the register TWICE and require the same answer. The update
      can begin between the check and the read; two equal reads
      either straddle no update, or both straddle the same junk --
      and the range check below catches the second case.
   3. Reject anything a second cannot be. Seconds are 0..59. A value
      outside that is not a late clock, it is a bad read, and the
      caller must be told so rather than handed a number.

 The mode bit is honoured: Status B bit 2 set means the registers are
 binary, clear means they are BCD. Most parts ship BCD and every one
 we have met does, but a reader that assumes it will silently mangle
 a machine that does not, and this one is cheap to get right.

 An unreadable clock returns -1. It is never an excuse to invent a
 time: the caller decides what to do without one, and that decision
 belongs to the caller.

  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 We say:

Section: Registers

  rtc-port-index : Integer = 112
  rtc-port-data : Integer = 113

  rtc-reg-seconds : Integer = 0
  rtc-reg-status-a : Integer = 10
  rtc-reg-status-b : Integer = 11

  rtc-uip-bit : Integer = 128
  rtc-binary-bit : Integer = 4

  rtc-uip-fuel : Integer = 1000000

  rtc-unreadable : Integer = -1

Section: Reading

  rtc-read : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  rtc-read (reg) = act
    w <- port-out-byte rtc-port-index reg
    v <- port-in-byte rtc-port-data
    v
  end

 Wait out the update. The fuel is generous because the window is
 short and a stall here is a stall in a poll loop, not in a driver;
 exhausting it means the clock is not answering, which is a reading
 in itself and is reported as one.

  rtc-wait-idle : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  rtc-wait-idle (fuel) = act
    a <- rtc-read rtc-reg-status-a
    if bit-and a rtc-uip-bit == 0 then 1
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else rtc-wait-idle (fuel - 1)
  end

  rtc-bcd-to-int : Integer -> Integer
  rtc-bcd-to-int (v) =
    let hi = bit-shru v 4
    in hi * 10 + bit-and v 15

  rtc-decode : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rtc-decode (v) (binary) =
    if binary /= 0 then v else rtc-bcd-to-int v

  rtc-plausible : Integer -> Integer
  rtc-plausible (s) =
    if s < 0 then rtc-unreadable
    else if s > 59 then rtc-unreadable
    else s

 The seconds, or -1. Three gates: the update must be over, two reads
 must agree, and the answer must be a second.

  rtc-seconds : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  rtc-seconds (d) = act
    idle <- rtc-wait-idle rtc-uip-fuel
    b <- rtc-read rtc-reg-status-b
    first <- rtc-read rtc-reg-seconds
    second <- rtc-read rtc-reg-seconds
    if idle == 0 then rtc-unreadable
    else if first /= second then rtc-unreadable
    else rtc-plausible (rtc-decode first (bit-and b rtc-binary-bit))
  end

 The unguarded read, kept ONLY so a test can demonstrate the hazard
 this chapter exists to close. Nothing in the boot path may call it.

  rtc-seconds-unguarded : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  rtc-seconds-unguarded (d) = act
    v <- rtc-read rtc-reg-seconds
    rtc-bcd-to-int v
  end
